### What is iterative binary search?

Iterative binary search is the loop-based implementation of the binary search algorithm. Instead of calling itself recursively, it uses a `while` loop that runs as long as the search range is valid. This makes it the preferred form for production code because it avoids recursion overhead and eliminates the risk of stack overflow on large arrays.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IterativeBinarySearch {

    public static int search(BigDecimal[] balances, BigDecimal target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = balances.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            int cmp = balances[mid].compareTo(target);

            if (cmp == 0) {
                return mid;
            } else if (cmp < 0) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] balances = {
            new BigDecimal("5000.00"),
            new BigDecimal("15000.00"),
            new BigDecimal("30000.00"),
            new BigDecimal("50000.00"),
            new BigDecimal("75000.00")
        };
        int idx = search(balances, new BigDecimal("30000.00"));
        System.out.println("Found at index: " + idx);
    }
}
```

```text
Found at index: 2
```

### Why prefer the iterative form over recursion?

The iterative form uses O(1) extra space. It stores only three integers: `left`, `right`, and `mid`. The recursive version creates a new stack frame for each halving step, consuming O(log n) space and risking a `StackOverflowError` if the array is extremely large.

In a banking system processing millions of sorted transaction IDs under tight memory constraints, the iterative form is the safe and efficient default.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class RecursiveVsIterative {

    // Recursive version: O(log n) stack space
    public static int recursiveSearch(int[] arr, int target, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) return -1;
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (arr[mid] < target) return recursiveSearch(arr, target, mid + 1, right);
        return recursiveSearch(arr, target, left, mid - 1);
    }

    // Iterative version: O(1) space
    public static int iterativeSearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;
            else right = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] txnIds = {101, 205, 310, 412, 520};
        System.out.println("Recursive: " + recursiveSearch(txnIds, 412, 0, txnIds.length - 1));
        System.out.println("Iterative: " + iterativeSearch(txnIds, 412));
    }
}
```

```text
Recursive: 3
Iterative: 3
```

### How does the loop template guarantee correctness?

The template `while (left <= right)` combined with `mid + 1` and `mid - 1` guarantees three things:

1. Every valid single-element range where `left == right` is checked.
2. The range strictly shrinks on every iteration because `mid` is always excluded.
3. The loop always terminates because the range shrinks by at least one each step.

If you use `while (left < right)` instead, you miss the case where the target is the single remaining element. If you forget the `+ 1` or `- 1`, the loop can spin forever.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class TemplateCorrectness {

    // WRONG: left < right misses single-element range
    public static int buggy(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left < right) { // BUG
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;
            else right = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    // CORRECT: left <= right
    public static int correct(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;
            else right = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30};
        System.out.println("Buggy search for 30:   " + buggy(arr, 30));
        System.out.println("Correct search for 30: " + correct(arr, 30));
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy search for 30:   -1
Correct search for 30: 2
```

### How do you handle an empty array or a single element?

When the array is empty, `left = 0` and `right = -1`, so `left <= right` is false and the loop never runs, correctly returning -1. When the array has a single element, `left = right = 0`, the loop runs once, checks `arr[0]`, and either matches or exits.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static int search(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;
            else right = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty array:   " + search(new int[]{}, 5));
        System.out.println("Single match:  " + search(new int[]{5}, 5));
        System.out.println("Single miss:   " + search(new int[]{3}, 5));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty array:   -1
Single match:  0
Single miss:   -1
```

### How do you adapt the template for lower-bound and upper-bound?

The leftmost-match (lower-bound) variant does not return early on a match. Instead it records the match and continues searching leftward. The rightmost-match (upper-bound) variant continues searching rightward.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class BoundarySearch {

    public static int lowerBound(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, result = -1;
        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                result = mid;
                right = mid - 1;  // keep searching left
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static int upperBound(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, result = -1;
        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                result = mid;
                left = mid + 1;  // keep searching right
            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {100, 200, 200, 200, 300, 400};
        System.out.println("First 200 at index: " + lowerBound(amounts, 200));
        System.out.println("Last 200 at index:  " + upperBound(amounts, 200));
    }
}
```

```text
First 200 at index: 1
Last 200 at index:  3
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Aspect | Value |
|---|---|
| Time complexity | O(log n) |
| Space complexity | O(1) |
| Best case | O(1) when the middle element is the target |
| Worst case | O(log n) when the target is at an edge or absent |
| Comparisons for n = 1,000,000 | At most 20 |

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class Complexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int n : new int[]{100, 10_000, 1_000_000}) {
            System.out.println("n=" + n + " max comparisons="
                + (int) Math.ceil(Math.log(n) / Math.log(2)));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
n=100 max comparisons=7
n=10000 max comparisons=14
n=1000000 max comparisons=20
```

### What are the common mistakes developers make?

The three classic bugs in iterative binary search:

1. Midpoint overflow: using `(left + right) / 2` instead of `left + (right - left) / 2`.
2. Wrong loop condition: using `<` instead of `<=`, which skips single-element ranges.
3. Missing pointer advancement: using `left = mid` instead of `left = mid + 1`, causing an infinite loop.

Each of these can silently produce wrong results or hang the program.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class InfiniteLoopBug {

    // WRONG: left = mid causes infinite loop when left == mid
    public static int infiniteLoop(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        int iterations = 0;
        while (left <= right) {
            iterations++;
            if (iterations > 100) {
                System.out.println("Stuck in infinite loop!");
                return -1;
            }
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid;  // BUG: should be mid + 1
            else right = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        infiniteLoop(arr, 50);
    }
}
```

```text
Stuck in infinite loop!
```

### When should a senior engineer choose iterative over recursive?

A senior engineer uses the iterative form as the default for all standard binary search lookups. The recursive form is reserved for problems where the recursion naturally models the structure, such as binary search tree validation where the bounds are passed to child calls. For the flat-array case, iterative is always preferred because it is constant space, cache-friendly, and has no overhead from function calls.

Java's own `Arrays.binarySearch` is iterative internally for exactly these reasons.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class ProductionChoice {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] sortedAccounts = new long[1_000_000];
        for (int i = 0; i < sortedAccounts.length; i++) {
            sortedAccounts[i] = 1000L + i;
        }

        long start = System.nanoTime();
        int idx = Arrays.binarySearch(sortedAccounts, 500_500L);
        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.println("Found at: " + idx);
        System.out.println("Time: " + elapsed + " ns");
    }
}
```

```text
Found at: 499500
Time: 1200 ns
```
